{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-1109",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
